<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Inditalk.Release.entitlements — entitlements for RELEASE builds (TestFlight + App Store).

  Identical to Inditalk.entitlements except `aps-environment` is `production`.

  Why two files: APNs is environment-split. A token minted against the sandbox gateway
  is rejected by the production gateway and vice versa, so a build signed with
  `development` silently receives no pushes once it ships through TestFlight or the App
  Store. Relying on Xcode to rewrite this during archive is fragile — it only happens
  with a Distribution profile that carries the Push capability, and a mismatch fails
  silently at runtime rather than at build time. Pinning it per configuration
  (CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS in project.yml) makes the behavior explicit and reproducible
  from the command line.

  PAIRED CONSOLE STEP: the same APNs .p8 auth key must be present in Firebase's
  PRODUCTION APNs slot (Project settings → Cloud Messaging → Apple app configuration).
  An auth key is environment-agnostic, so it is the SAME file you uploaded to the
  development slot — but Firebase keys its lookup by slot, so it must be uploaded to
  both. Without it, FCM cannot reach production APNs and pushes to release builds fail.

  Everything else follows Inditalk.entitlements: VoIP (PushKit) and CallKit need no extra
  entitlement key — they rely on the Push Notifications capability (this
  `aps-environment` key) plus the `voip` UIBackgroundMode declared in Info.plist.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>aps-environment</key>
    <string>production</string>
</dict>
</plist>
